4. Are There Natural-Sourced Melatonin Supplements? 
Some supplements claim to be derived from natural sources, such as: 
•	Animal pineal glands (e.g., bovine) – Rare today due to safety and disease concerns. 
•	Plant-based melatonin – Some companies extract melatonin from plants like rice or herbs, though the amounts are much lower and more expensive to produce. 
Most “natural” melatonin on the market is still synthetically manufactured, but designed to match the molecule of natural melatonin exactly. 
5. When to Use Synthetic Melatonin 
Synthetic melatonin is often used in adults (including those aged 25–57) for: 
•	Jet lag recovery 
•	Delayed Sleep Phase Syndrome 
•	Insomnia 
•	Shift work sleep disorder 
•	Supporting sleep in conditions like anxiety or stress-related insomnia 
It is generally non-habit-forming, but should be used under guidance, especially in people with hormonal or mood disorders. 
