This analysis is based on general trends from the collected responses. 
1. Sleep Patterns and Habits 
•	Bedtime Trends: 
Around 32.9% of respondents reported going to bed between between 10 pm to 11pm, which aligns reasonably well with the body’s natural circadian rhythm.  
However, a notable 61.6%% sleep after 11 PM, indicating a delayed sleep schedule, possibly influenced by extended screen time or work patterns. Such delays can result in a mismatch between melatonin secretion and actual sleep onset, leading to sleep debt over time. 
•	Sleep Duration: 
The majority i.e 67.1% sleep between 6 to 8 hours per night, within the recommended range for adults. However, 30.1% sleep less than 6 hours, which can lead to chronic fatigue, impaired concentration, and weakened immune function—symptoms often linked to melatonin dysregulation. 
 
•	Sleep Onset & Difficulty: 
Approximately 56.2 % reported difficulty falling asleep, and 19.1% noted taking more than 30 minutes to fall asleep. This delay in sleep initiation is a strong indicator of circadian disruption, possibly linked to light exposure, stress, or irregular schedules. 
 
•	Sleep Quality: 
On a 1–5 rating scale, most participants i.e 46.6% chose 3 (average) or lower, suggesting that even with adequate sleep duration, the quality of rest is lacking. Poor sleep quality often correlates with inconsistent melatonin rhythms or late-night screen habits. 
 
 
 
2. Screen Exposure and Blue Light Impact 
•	Total Daily Screen Time: 
A significant 50.7% of respondents spend over 4 hours daily on screens (phones, computers, TVs). With digital work, entertainment, and social interaction now screencentric, this level of exposure is almost unavoidable—but it comes at a cost to melatonin balance, especially if unfiltered blue light is involved. 
 
•	Screens Before Bed: 
Nearly 87.7% admitted to using screens within 1 hour before bedtime—a critical period when melatonin should rise. Blue light from screens is known to suppress melatonin production, delay the onset of drowsiness, and interfere with the body’s readiness for sleep. This finding is one of the clearest behavioral links to the sleep difficulties observed. 
 
•	Night Mode Usage: 
46,6% use night mode always and 32.9% use night mode sometimes.  Only a moderate proportion of respondents use night mode or blue light filters. While night mode can reduce blue light exposure, its inconsistent use limits its effectiveness in preserving melatonin rhythms. 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
3. Awareness and Supplement Use 
•	Awareness of Melatonin: 
About 76.7%  of respondents reported having heard of melatonin, showing basic public familiarity. However, awareness did not always translate into understanding its function or how behavior affects its production. 
 
•	Knowledge of Light’s Role: 
Slightly more than 75.3% were aware that screen light affects melatonin, indicating  understanding of the biological consequences of screen habits.  
 
 
4. Daytime Effects 
•	Feeling Refreshed: 
Only 28.8%  of respondents consistently felt refreshed in the morning, while the rest reported grogginess or the need for stimulants like caffeine. This suggests that even when sleep occurs, it may not be deep or restorative, likely due to poor melatonin alignment with sleep phases. 
 
•	Daytime Sleepiness: 
A total of 93.2% experience daytime sleepiness, a classic symptom of circadian misalignment and poor sleep quality. This can result in reduced productivity, mood fluctuations, and long-term health consequences. 
 
 
 
 

 
CONCLUSION: 
The survey highlights a clear connection between modern digital habits and disruption of natural sleep cycles, with melatonin playing a central role. While general awareness of melatonin exists, behavioral patterns such as late-night screen exposure and inadequate sleep hygiene continue to hinder its effectiveness. For healthier sleep outcomes, there is a growing need to prioritize lifestyle changes over reliance on supplements. By adopting practical measures—like reducing screen use before bed, maintaining consistent sleep schedules, and creating darker, tech-free sleep environments—individuals can support their body’s natural melatonin production and enhance overall well-being. This project underscores the importance of aligning our habits with our biology to achieve truly restorative sleep. 
 
 
 
 
 
 
